out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Rim was asked how she ensures quality and answered it exactly as asked: we have procedures and we follow them. Which is true and is worth nothing, and she knew that while she was saying it — the question had no occasion in it, so the answer had no figure in it, which is 13.1 from the other end. The something to do about it is to name the two questions and let them choose: if what you are asking is whether we have procedures, then yes and I can send them; if you are asking what proportion of what leaves this depot has been checked against them, then it is thirty-one of forty last quarter and I can tell you about the nine. Rim said the second is the one she wants to answer, and of course it is, and that is precisely why both must be offered: a reframing that offers one answer is not a reframing but a substitution, and the panel has noticed. Dima, who has sat on the other side of this, said a panel that is corrected stops listening to the content and starts listening to the manner, and you cannot get them back in the time available — it is not that they are offended, it is that you have given them a second thing to assess and they only had time for the first. The cost of correcting is not the offence; it is the attention. Adnan asked how often you can do it, and once: twice in one interview and you are a person who finds questions imprecise, which is a true thing about you that you should not be advertising. And Jad asked what happens afterwards, which is the part everybody forgets: after you reframe you must answer the question they asked, the one you set aside, in one sentence at the end. Otherwise you have not answered two questions; you have dodged one. Bassel said that feels like the opposite of what reframing is for, and it is what makes reframing honest rather than clever: anybody can redirect a question, and the move that costs something is redirecting it and then coming back to pay for it. And Mazen asked about the tone, since it can sound like a lecture, and the answer is in one feature — put a rise on the end of the reframing and not a fall. A rise makes it a request for permission to answer the second one; a fall makes it an announcement that the second one is the real question. Same words, and only one of the two is survivable.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the partner\'s two questions and their return sentence. ⚠ **Collect the returns and read four of them aloud with no other context.** They are the most revealing sentences the class produces in this unit, because **a return is the one sentence a candidate has no incentive to say**, and a room can hear that. **Then the two words that settle the cycle**: not _rude_, but **attention** — **a corrected panel has been given a second thing to assess and had time for one.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _That is the wrong question._ → **✓** offer two answers and let them choose.   **✗** _What I think you really mean is…_ → **✓** never tell somebody what they mean.   **✗** Reframing and then not returning. → **✓** one sentence at the end.   **✗** Reframing a question that takes a figure. → **✓** answer it.   **✗** Reframing twice. → **✓** once; the second bad question goes straight.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **None of them can take a figure**, which means none can be answered well — and **six of the nine answered them as asked**, producing six interchangeable answers she can no longer attribute to individual candidates. **She is explicit that this is a description of what the question did, not a criticism of the six.**'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **Both lost the room inside a sentence** — **not because the panel was offended, but because it started attending to how they were speaking.** **The panel had come to assess one thing and was suddenly assessing two.**'));
out.push(A('**3** _**And on the first, yes, the procedures exist.**_ ⚠ **The reframing is a technique and three people could have been taught it in an afternoon. The return is a disposition.** **She had set the question aside and came back and paid for it**, and nobody does that who is only trying to get through the room.'));
