// ===================== 13.4 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('13.4  The question you ask them'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **The two columns are the cycle.** In most classes **the _what_ column is three or four times the length of the _how_ column**, and several of the _how_ questions turn out on inspection to be _what_ questions with _how_ at the front. **Count both and leave the count on the board.**'));
out.push(A('**b** **2** and **4** would change a panel\'s mind. Not **1**, **3** or **5** — ⚠ **all three have predictable answers**, and a question whose answer you can predict is **a courtesy with a question mark on it.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — **a question about how something works, which only somebody who has thought about the job can ask** — and at the end it is the last thing they hear.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** _Is there training?_ ⚠ **For two years, every time.** **It did nothing because the answer was always going to be yes.**   **2** ⚠ **Never.** **It is the only move in an interview that can be detected with certainty**, because the panel watches you receive the answer — **and nobody receives known information convincingly.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The one-year question is the single most transferable thing in Unit 13 and students should leave with it written down.** _What would have to be true in a year for you to say this appointment had worked?_ **It makes a panel state criteria out loud, often for the first time**, and it works whether or not they can answer it: ⚠ **a panel that cannot say what success would look like has not decided what the post is for**, and the candidate now knows something no reference would have told them. **Give the class the whole of that second half**, because otherwise they will treat a bad answer as a failed question.'));
out.push(A('**The risk criterion is what separates this from the advice students have read elsewhere, and it should be stated as a test.** ⚠ **A question you would not mind either answer to is a question you did not need to ask.** Stage 4 enforces it: **both answers written down, and then whether they would still want the post after the one they did not want.** **If the answer is yes to both, the question is decoration** — and about a third of a class will discover that about their own first attempt. ⚠ **That discovery is the cycle working**, so do not rescue them from it.'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(how a decision reaches the decider)_ _How does a request like mine actually reach the person who signs it off?_',
  '**2** _(what the last person did first)_ _When this post has been filled well before, what did that person do in their first three months?_',
  '**3** _(the one-year question)_ _What would have to be true in a year for you to say this appointment had worked?_',
  '**4** _(a follow-up to an answer I did not like)_ _So is the difficulty producing the document, or getting a decision about it?_ ⚠ **One follow-up, then stop.**',
  '**5** _(one I would not mind either answer to)_ _Is there a staff canteen?_ ⚠ **I would not ask it. Either answer leaves me exactly where I was**, which is the test.',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Mark these on **whether the question could be asked by somebody who had not thought about the job.** If it could, it is not doing the work. Item **4** is the discipline: **one follow-up and one only**, and a narrowing is the best use of it. Item **5** is the one to collect: ⚠ **make every student produce a question they would not ask and say why**, because the test is easier to apply to a rejected question than to a cherished one.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
